# Sidekick Work: settings for the work-laptop package (docs/start/work-laptop.md, docs/start/work-laptop.sv.md).
# Copy to work.env next to this file (gitignored) and edit, or let `bash tools/work.sh setup` ask the questions and
# write the file for you (it can be run again; it only rewrites the settings it asks about).
#
# No secrets here. Database passwords, session secret and the optional model API key are generated or asked for by
# `bash tools/work.sh init` and kept in deploy/work/secrets/ (directory mode 700, files mode 600, gitignored).
#
# Only these names are read from this file. They are NOT passed on to the containers as they are: the compose
# overlay maps them to Sidekick's own settings, so a typo here cannot enable anything else.

# --- Memory (optional) ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# off (default) = Sidekick runs entirely on the laptop, with no model server: Hindsight and the memory worker are
# not started and nothing needs to be reachable. on = turn the memory on; it needs the model server below.
# `bash tools/work.sh setup` asks. An older work.env without this line keeps memory on.
SIDEKICK_WORK_MEMORY=off

# --- The company model server (vLLM, OpenAI-compatible), only needed when SIDEKICK_WORK_MEMORY=on ---------------
# Base URL including /v1, exactly as OpenCode and similar tools are configured. It is the ONLY address the
# installation can reach. http or https; for https the laptop's own trust store is used (a company CA installed
# on Ubuntu works). A host name or an IP address both work. Replace the placeholder; start refuses it as is when memory is on.
SIDEKICK_WORK_MODEL_BASE_URL=http://<vllm-host>:<port>/v1

# Only if the host name above does not resolve inside Docker (for example a name that exists only in the
# laptop's /etc/hosts, or a VPN-only DNS): the IP the gateway should connect to instead.
# SIDEKICK_WORK_MODEL_RESOLVE_IP=10.0.0.12

# --- More addresses Sidekick may reach (allowlist) ------------------------------------------------------------
# Besides the model server (if memory is on), nothing is reachable. Add host names with a port, comma separated, to allow more
# (for example a separate embeddings server). Host names only, one distinct port each; the client keeps using the
# real name and its own certificate check. Everything else stays blocked.
# SIDEKICK_WORK_ALLOW=embed.company.lan:443

# --- Model (id exactly as vLLM lists them: `bash tools/work.sh status` prints them from GET <base>/models) ----
# Hindsight's language model (retain and reflect), required when memory is on. A fast, cheap model is a good fit. Sidekick itself
# calls no language model.
SIDEKICK_WORK_MEMORY_MODEL=<model-id-for-deepseek-v4.1-flash>

# --- Embeddings and reranker (Hindsight memory) ----------------------------------------------------------------
# local (default) = the models that ship inside the Hindsight image; nothing leaves the laptop and nothing is
# downloaded. openai = ask an /embeddings endpoint on the SAME model server instead (Hindsight provider
# `openai`). Decide before the first start: stored vectors cannot be compared with another model's vectors.
# SIDEKICK_WORK_EMBEDDINGS_PROVIDER=local
# SIDEKICK_WORK_EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=<embedding-model-id>

# --- Instance ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The name Claude shows for this connector, so work and private Sidekick are never mixed up.
SIDEKICK_WORK_INSTANCE_LABEL=Sidekick Work
# Language of the apps and of the model's answers: sv (Swedish, the default) or en (English). Setup asks for it.
SIDEKICK_WORK_LOCALE=sv
SIDEKICK_WORK_TIMEZONE=Europe/Stockholm
# Local port on 127.0.0.1 (the MCP address becomes http://127.0.0.1:<port>/mcp).
SIDEKICK_WORK_PORT=8765

# Rooms to switch on, comma separated: work, journal, shopping, contacts, control. The list must include `work`, or
# Sidekick does not start. The proposal for a work machine is every room except shopping; `bash tools/work.sh setup`
# asks which rooms you want, so choose the rooms this instance should offer.
# work: Work — agenda, status board, domains, routines/processes, projects, tasks and steps (required).
# journal: Journal — dated entries, journal sources and draft review.
# shopping: Shopping — shopping lists, items and purchase check-offs.
# contacts: Contacts — people, contact details and links to work records.
# control: Control — trash/restore, recent changes and derived-memory controls.
# A disabled room loses its navigation and MCP tools (and its data is excluded from cross-room reads);
# existing data is kept. For example: SIDEKICK_ENABLED_ROOMS=work,contacts,control
# Remove the line (or answer - in the guide) to switch every room on. The api and the stdio process Claude Desktop
# starts both read this value.
SIDEKICK_ENABLED_ROOMS=work,journal,contacts,control

# Rarely needed: another CA bundle than the Ubuntu one, and another image tag than the one built locally.
# SIDEKICK_WORK_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
# SIDEKICK_WORK_API_IMAGE=roadlake-sidekick:work
